<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otepää</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otepää is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, built beside a hillfort that was the main stronghold of the ancient Estonian county of Ugandi; its name means 'bear's head'. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> of the early 13th century the fort changed hands several times between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> and Russian princes, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> built a stone castle there. Today it is a winter sports resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otepaa.jpg" alt="Otepää" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivo Kruusamägi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otep%C3%A4%C3%A4_-_wmee-007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Otepää lies in Valga County in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, in the hilly, lake-filled region known as the Otepää Upland. At up to 171 metres above sea level it is the highest of Estonia's towns, and Lake Pühajärv lies nearby. The name means Ott's Head in South Estonian, where ott is a euphemism for bear. The town was formerly called Nuustaku and is nicknamed the winter capital of Estonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlement dates to the sixth century BCE, and the site has been inhabited continuously since the sixth or seventh century CE. It was the site of an Ugandi County hill fort. Rus' sources first mention the fortress in 1116, when the princes of Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> and Otepää. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a> began with an attack on the fortress in 1208, and in 1217 Christianized southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> stopped the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> there. German crusaders finally conquered the fort in 1224.</p><p>Hermann of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, the first Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of the Bishopric of Dorpat, built an episcopal castle there, the first stone fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Its importance declined in the fourteenth century as Tartu, the bishopric's seat, grew. It is commonly believed that the Livonian Order razed the castle in 1396 during a conflict with the bishopric, although archaeologists have argued that it was inhabited as late as 1477. The earliest surviving firearm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was found in the castle and dates to at least 1396.</p><p>The settlement was named Nuustaku and given town privileges in 1862. The first agricultural fair was held at the church manor in 1876, and on 4 June 1884 the flag that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>'s national flag was dedicated in Nuustaku Church as the flag of the Estonian Students' Society. The name changed to Otepää in 1922, and it officially became a town in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>St Mary's Church stands in the town; its oldest parts date to the 1860s, and it was largely rebuilt in 1889 and 1890. Otepää is a popular skiing resort and hosted FIS Cross-Country World Cup events from the 2005 to 2006 season.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otepää” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Otepää</b>, whose name means 'bear's head', was its main stronghold, and Tartu on the Emajõgi river guarded the waterway east to Lake Peipus.</blockquote><a href="/state/ugandi">Ugandi</a></li></ul></section>
